1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,458
April fourteenth, nineteen twelve, one forty two in the afternoon.

2
00:00:03,458 --> 00:00:06,896
A captain hands the ship's owner a warning about ice ahead.

3
00:00:06,896 --> 00:00:09,084
He folds it into his coat pocket.

4
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:14,439
Two hours after midnight, that ship is at the bottom of the Atlantic.

5
00:00:14,439 --> 00:00:20,264
More than fifteen hundred people are dead, and two separate governments open investigations to find out exactly why.

6
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:25,890
Two governments investigated this same disaster. They read the same testimony.

7
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:27,930
They still reached different verdicts.

8
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:32,921
Both inquiries are real. Both reports are public record, still readable today.

9
00:00:32,921 --> 00:00:37,739
And on at least one point, the two governments do not agree with each other.

10
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:40,657
This is not the movie version.

11
00:00:40,657 --> 00:00:47,225
This is what the two official investigations actually found, a hundred and fourteen years ago, and where they still contradict each other.

12
00:00:48,366 --> 00:00:50,325
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

13
00:00:51,466 --> 00:00:54,940
Titanic. The verdict two governments couldn't agree on.

14
00:00:56,066 --> 00:01:00,115
Chapter one. The ship with more lifeboats than the law required.

15
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:06,170
White Star Line built this ship to answer a rival's speed record, not to break one.

16
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:11,107
Where Cunard's liners were built to be fast, White Star built for size, and for comfort.

17
00:01:12,233 --> 00:01:16,030
Construction took twenty six months at the Harland and Wolff yard in Belfast.

18
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:18,659
Eight of the yard's own workers died building her.

19
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:26,293
A trade journal at the time called the ship's watertight compartment system, quote, practically unsinkable.

20
00:01:26,293 --> 00:01:29,323
That is a real, qualified engineering claim.

21
00:01:29,323 --> 00:01:34,950
The flat, unqualified word unsinkable came later, mostly from newspapers, after the fact.

22
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:43,178
That popular idea, that this ship was marketed to the public as unsinkable, is mostly a myth invented after it sank.

23
00:01:44,299 --> 00:01:47,844
Here is the real flaw, and it is not the one you think.

24
00:01:47,844 --> 00:01:57,387
British lifeboat law scaled the number of boats a ship had to carry to its weight in tons, and that law had not been updated since ships a third this size were the biggest afloat.

25
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:02,470
Under that outdated law, this ship needed sixteen lifeboats. It carried twenty.

26
00:02:02,470 --> 00:02:09,687
It was carrying more boats than the law required, and its boats still had room for barely half the people on board.

27
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:15,032
On the day itself, seven separate ice warnings reached this ship's radio room.

28
00:02:15,032 --> 00:02:17,616
One came from the steamer Caronia that morning.

29
00:02:17,616 --> 00:02:24,077
One came from the Baltic that afternoon, and that is the one the captain personally carried to the ship's owner.

30
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:30,195
One more warning came in just before eight PM that night, from a ship called the Mesaba.

31
00:02:30,195 --> 00:02:34,310
It described a solid field of ice and icebergs directly in this ship's path.

32
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:39,306
That message never reached the bridge. It was left behind on a table in the radio room.

33
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:47,120
By eleven that night, the ship was still holding roughly twenty two knots, through a night the crew already knew was full of ice.

34
00:02:48,266 --> 00:02:52,864
Chapter two. Seven warnings. One never reached the bridge.

35
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,212
A few miles away, another ship, the Californian, had already stopped for the night.

36
00:02:58,212 --> 00:03:02,124
Surrounded by ice, her captain had gone to sleep in the chart room.

37
00:03:03,266 --> 00:03:08,589
At around eleven PM, the Californian's radio operator tapped out a message to this ship.

38
00:03:08,589 --> 00:03:11,782
We are stopped, he said, and surrounded by ice.

39
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:17,484
This ship's own senior radio operator, buried in passenger telegrams, cut him off.

40
00:03:17,484 --> 00:03:22,068
Shut up, he sent back. I am busy. I am working Cape Race.

41
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,033
Minutes later, the Californian's operator switched his radio off for the night, and went to bed.

42
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:35,149
At eleven forty PM, high in the crow's nest, a lookout named Frederick Fleet saw something directly ahead.

43
00:03:35,149 --> 00:03:38,805
He rang the warning bell three times, and called the bridge.

44
00:03:39,933 --> 00:03:41,657
Iceberg, right ahead.

45
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:48,129
Chapter three. Eleven forty PM. The night the ship had left itself no margin.

46
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:53,720
There is no photograph of what happened in the next few seconds. No camera was there.

47
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:58,174
This is a reconstruction, built only from the testimony of the men who lived through it.

48
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:03,006
The iceberg did not smash through the hull in one blow.

49
00:04:03,006 --> 00:04:09,409
It scraped along the side, below the waterline, popping rivets and buckling steel plates across several compartments at once.

50
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:13,965
That was the one failure this ship's design could not survive.

51
00:04:13,965 --> 00:04:18,334
It was built to stay afloat with any four of its watertight compartments flooded.

52
00:04:18,334 --> 00:04:21,766
That night, water was pouring into six of them at once.

53
00:04:22,899 --> 00:04:30,387
Captain Stanley Lord, on the Californian, was woken twice that night about strange lights, and rockets, in the distance.

54
00:04:30,387 --> 00:04:36,692
Asked later what color they were, he testified, quote, I do not know. I was asleep.

55
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:43,325
On board, the ship had lifeboat capacity for a little over half the people it carried that night.

56
00:04:43,325 --> 00:04:45,461
Some boats were lowered only partly full.

57
00:04:46,599 --> 00:04:52,410
By two twenty in the morning, the ship was gone. Survival split sharply by class.

58
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:55,896
Roughly ninety seven percent of first class women survived.

59
00:04:55,896 --> 00:05:00,156
Roughly a quarter of third class passengers, of either sex, did.

60
00:05:01,299 --> 00:05:08,684
Before dawn, a Cunard liner called the Carpathia reached the lifeboats and picked up just over seven hundred survivors.

61
00:05:08,684 --> 00:05:14,125
This is that actual ship, real footage, filmed steaming toward them that same night.

62
00:05:15,266 --> 00:05:21,328
Neither official inquiry ever formally investigated why the survival gap by class was so wide.

63
00:05:21,328 --> 00:05:22,944
The disparity is documented.

64
00:05:22,944 --> 00:05:28,197
Its exact cause, to this day, was never formally settled by either government.

65
00:05:29,333 --> 00:05:35,029
Casualty counts still differ by source, from around fourteen hundred ninety, to fifteen hundred seventeen.

66
00:05:35,029 --> 00:05:39,207
Even the death toll, both governments could not fully agree on.

67
00:05:40,333 --> 00:05:50,286
Days later, a real recovery ship, the Mackay-Bennett, left Halifax and searched the water where the wreckage had drifted, for the dead the lifeboats never reached.

68
00:05:51,433 --> 00:05:54,907
Chapter four. Two governments. Two verdicts.

69
00:05:56,033 --> 00:05:59,674
Within days, the United States Senate opened its own inquiry.

70
00:05:59,674 --> 00:06:05,135
Weeks later, so did the British government, in what's known as the Wreck Commissioner's inquiry.

71
00:06:05,135 --> 00:06:07,684
Both are real documents. Both still exist.

72
00:06:08,833 --> 00:06:10,482
This is the British report.

73
00:06:10,482 --> 00:06:18,070
Its actual verdict on the ship's captain was not guilty of negligence, because he was simply following the accepted practice of the time.

74
00:06:18,070 --> 00:06:20,379
But the inquiry condemned that practice itself.

75
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:25,322
The American inquiry reached a harsher verdict on the same captain.

76
00:06:25,322 --> 00:06:32,968
His own indifference to danger, the Senate report concluded, was one of the direct causes of what it called an unnecessary tragedy.

77
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:39,088
Two governments studied the same man, on the same night, and reached two different verdicts.

78
00:06:40,233 --> 00:06:41,817
Then there is the Californian.

79
00:06:41,817 --> 00:06:52,589
The British report's real finding was specific: that ship, it concluded, could have pushed through the ice to open water, and might have saved many, if not all, of the lives that were lost.

80
00:06:53,733 --> 00:06:59,846
The American inquiry went further, recommending, quote, drastic action against that ship's captain.

81
00:07:00,966 --> 00:07:04,316
That captain spent the rest of his life disputing it.

82
00:07:04,316 --> 00:07:08,337
He died in nineteen sixty two, still fighting to clear his name.

83
00:07:08,337 --> 00:07:14,367
Two separate petitions to reopen the case, in nineteen sixty five and nineteen sixty eight, were both rejected.

84
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,482
Researchers still argue about it today.

85
00:07:17,482 --> 00:07:26,071
Some maintain a third, unidentified ship was sitting between the two vessels that night, and that the Californian was never as close as either inquiry concluded.

86
00:07:26,071 --> 00:07:32,349
That theory is disputed, not settled, and it belongs to the people who argue it, not to this report.

87
00:07:33,466 --> 00:07:37,846
The ship's owner faced his own verdict, in the newspapers rather than either report.

88
00:07:37,846 --> 00:07:43,164
He survived by boarding a lifeboat, and the press spent years calling him a coward for it.

89
00:07:43,164 --> 00:07:46,292
His own testimony says he helped load others nearby, first.

90
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:50,823
Neither official government report ever called him a coward.

91
00:07:50,823 --> 00:07:54,590
That verdict belonged to the newspapers, not to either investigation.

92
00:07:55,733 --> 00:07:59,364
Chapter five. What changed, because of this ship.

93
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:03,893
Both reports agreed on enough to force real change.

94
00:08:03,893 --> 00:08:09,173
Within two years, thirteen nations signed the first international treaty on safety at sea.

95
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:16,370
Every ship, from then on, had to carry enough lifeboats for every person aboard, regardless of its tonnage.

96
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:20,079
Every ship had to keep its radio staffed around the clock.

97
00:08:20,079 --> 00:08:25,138
And a new ice patrol was created, to track icebergs in the shipping lanes, permanently.

98
00:08:26,266 --> 00:08:30,380
The wireless operator who cut off that final warning died in the sinking.

99
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:37,342
The one who sent it survived, avoided the spotlight for the rest of his life, and died quietly in nineteen fifty six.

100
00:08:38,466 --> 00:08:42,607
The ship's owner lived another twenty five years, largely out of public life.

101
00:08:42,607 --> 00:08:46,747
The Californian's captain spent his final years still fighting the finding against him.

102
00:08:47,866 --> 00:08:57,113
The wreck itself was not found for another seventy three years, until nineteen eighty five, during what was, at the time, a secret American Navy mission.

103
00:08:58,233 --> 00:09:03,457
A submersible dove to that same wreck in twenty twenty three, and never came back.

104
00:09:03,457 --> 00:09:06,592
This channel has already told that story in full.

105
00:09:07,733 --> 00:09:11,689
A famous film, decades later, gave the world its picture of this night.

106
00:09:11,689 --> 00:09:16,559
Its own director has said he made it as accurate as he could, at the time.

107
00:09:16,559 --> 00:09:20,820
But even that film never puts the two governments' real, conflicting verdicts on screen.

108
00:09:21,966 --> 00:09:25,500
A hundred and fourteen years later, both reports are still public record.

109
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:27,266
Both are still available to read.

110
00:09:27,266 --> 00:09:31,683
And on the question of who could have been saved, they still do not agree.

111
00:09:32,833 --> 00:09:35,048
What stays with me here isn't the iceberg.

112
00:09:35,048 --> 00:09:39,755
It's that a ship carrying more lifeboats than the law demanded still didn't have enough of them.

113
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:47,169
And that two governments investigated the same night, read the same testimony, and still walked away with different answers.

114
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:52,563
A hundred and fourteen years is a long time for a verdict to still be disputed.

115
00:09:52,563 --> 00:09:53,629
This one still is.

116
00:09:54,766 --> 00:10:05,372
If two governments quietly disagreeing on the same disaster got you, wait until you see how the official Chernobyl report reversed its own verdict six years after the fact.

117
00:10:05,372 --> 00:10:07,200
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

